id "4b44c854-917d-5ba0-b5a2-d41308ca5256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158158 #edb/ref 1061 27841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61e344b0-9976-54f7-a03c-175fe1505fbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1061 27850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd4dd871-25f7-54f5-9345-a556f2356cb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158167 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1061 27932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3576e13c-4960-5e5a-b072-2acaff8161cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158170 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1064 "A **Poisson distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nIf the rate of the events is $\\lambda,$ then the number of events $x$ occurring in an interval of fixed length has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,3, \\ldots\n$$\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a Poisson distribution with rate $\\lambda,$ then we write $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda).$\n\nFor example, suppose that calls come in at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute at a call center. Then the number of calls $X$ that are received during the next minute has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3^x e^{-3}}{x!}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the probability of receiving $4$ calls during the next minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{4}{e}^{-3}}{4!} \\\\  & ≈0.168\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1065 4525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6e07ef85-f3ca-52c8-89e2-062d8ea7c9eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158491 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1064 "The shape of the graph of the Poisson distribution depends on the value of $\\lambda.$ When $\\lambda$ is small, the probability is concentrated at small values of $x.$ When $\\lambda$ is larger, the probability is concentrated at larger values of $x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8af371733bd248a4e5ba3bc35199f00414188e9ca033a04dee2a7de4b0a7db4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1065 4534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f0026198-16d4-5332-9f5b-036bd00ef32d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158493 #edb/ref 1067 "The Graph of the Poisson Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428777 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428777 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2838->3282. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [